  {
    "_ulid": "01KJDQ1B9C62DT55EK8WK01BP0",
    "slugs": [],
    "title": "Plan import warnings treated as errors in batch mode. The no YAML code block in specs section warnin",
    "type": "task",
    "description": "**What**: When `kspec plan import` runs inside batch mode, non-fatal warnings (e.g., 'no YAML code block in specs section') cause the batch to fail and roll back. The plan-import command uses `warn()` for non-fatal issues and `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` on completion. In batch mode, `process.exit()` is intercepted and throws `BatchExitError`. The exit code 0 is correctly detected as success (line 859 in batch-exec.ts). However, the warnings written to stderr via `warn()` are captured by `OutputCapture` and when the captured output is returned, it may be treated as an error indicator by the batch result processing.\n\nThe core issue is that `plan import` calls `process.exit(0)` even on success (line 687 of plan-import.ts). In batch mode, this throws `BatchExitError(0)`, which IS handled as success (batch-exec.ts line 859). But the warnings in captured stderr make the output look like a failure. Additionally, the batch atomic mode may see stderr output as part of the error string when `BatchExitError` code is non-zero for other plan import failures.\n\n**Where**:\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` line 687 — `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` which triggers BatchExitError in batch mode\n- `src/cli/commands/plan-import.ts` lines 124-135 — warning emission for non-fatal parse errors\n- `src/cli/batch-exec.ts` lines 849-875 — BatchExitError handling in `dispatchCommand()`\n- `src/cli/batch-context.ts` — OutputCapture that captures both stdout and stderr\n\n**How**:\n1. In `plan-import.ts`, replace the final `process.exit(EXIT_CODES.SUCCESS)` with a simple `return`. The batch executor expects normal returns for success — only use `process.exit()` for failures
```
